#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119592 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/id #uuid "c5560ec9-32d6-514d-92fa-fbd77500d943" :question/math-academy-id "e-4431" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/fc/fcc4c2fb5ffaf79bd40a967fa0f3ff3ed3c25a4bc91e831f679aa2162d93050b.svg) find ![](images/3a/3a950a6005df11dc3df83b40a796c8e5ce962b591e040207bb961da532ec92dd.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given two ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrices ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) and ![](images/50/504b9ae6bd4255a78e26a2d32aaca2d613171ad01c9ac5d992f5512d093605e7.svg) we have\n\n![](images/3f/3fbf04c7a536c85422f2762eb5475ff5edfcff7a71eee0fa3cc14143e79bb10f.svg)\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n![](images/16/16c31a7e010da9d8f4e17628bc1c7b5dd3ef1d65a4e5e191bec2fd404135a7bc.svg)\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we notice that it's upper triangular. Therefore, the determinant is simply the product of the diagonal entries.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg)st column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n![](images/27/273e8879a6f78121b98902852e974aef15d9408e7cd026d3cc17e51837248ede.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "17487f8f-4974-5aaf-8729-6b1336ca5eaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-4432" :question/problem "Find $|A^{-1}|,$ if  ![](images/8e/8e92cd7ddbecf627452e4fa63d2af25fc60fad228bf42b5c4f8c9dfad28f4971.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|A^{-1}| = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 14 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-12+7) \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) =  \\dfrac{1}{|A|} = -\\dfrac{1}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "d5d020cb-50ee-53f6-9d8b-d384bc9b7999" :question/math-academy-id "e-4433" :question/problem "Given that \n![](images/58/589324d1b345360020e9c2b1096b64a08008a52b05a13fdc7fd39bcb063a8d58.svg)\nfind \n![](images/f4/f4925a93ca952863cac520a853c1bc5d7c815dc8324043d6c0963d20d2b2b1ff.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-10}+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0c86ff-0d80-5008-95a2-bec3a1c1a808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420799} {:answer/id #uuid "11030227-d4ce-5b70-8894-186891176d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420800} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd0e1b8-d837-556e-96c2-a100f45e8fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420816} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fdf14b-3a08-5a96-9420-377d1a8d1538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420822} {:answer/id #uuid "daea1037-0203-5328-a357-a7d37817bb29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dd0e1b8-d837-556e-96c2-a100f45e8fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420816} :answer-field/id #uuid "57142131-309b-5e63-93f0-085dd8ef09bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1079762-fa86-56ef-a06d-de94245b17fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-130474" :question/problem "Given that $C=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(C).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(C)=det\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (-1)(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(6-4)\\cdot (-1)(12-5) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "255676dd-836e-5694-abf7-d5d8904ac9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-224" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263324} {:answer/id #uuid "2eda70ee-d361-5fce-9c20-c0b3d369e65a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263395} {:answer/id #uuid "b5361b1e-013d-5ce9-82f8-9cb252305b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264563} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ee1395-9652-5dad-99e0-df830e63634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264890} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf0bcdb-ba33-5e2c-8a4d-88e00d25e056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2eda70ee-d361-5fce-9c20-c0b3d369e65a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263395} :answer-field/id #uuid "eab2e233-dfa3-53c1-8cbc-d36565b65180" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e431c517-0c91-5e86-97df-a91a6c031bdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-130484" :question/problem "Given that $Q=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 9 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(Q).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(Q)=det\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 9 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =(4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-1))\\cdot (3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(-20)\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =240.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64aeb222-7918-5204-aca3-b5f7c67d0aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572947} {:answer/id #uuid "a7af22e2-842e-5201-82af-fd7103681cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573036} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c15b8d-c591-5a9f-abf6-377fe85a7254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573054} {:answer/id #uuid "ba3522c1-f421-5648-aa43-188c5577d31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573062} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0a93da-9016-5e38-9f8e-6fcc94e3a5b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3c15b8d-c591-5a9f-abf6-377fe85a7254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573054} :answer-field/id #uuid "7192d7f2-ef6b-5f63-9173-1d9eab04f2ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ebd673b-ab24-50e2-a8c2-fa7ddd43f728" :question/math-academy-id "q-130491" :question/problem "Given that $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -1\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(D).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =((-2)\\cdot 1\\cdot 6)\\cdot (3\\cdot 1\\cdot -1) \\\\  & =(-12)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14df6deb-d3ee-5e1a-ad59-ff7cc32a5d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263194} {:answer/id #uuid "5cad807e-0569-5ff3-9e40-730b8b912410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263801} {:answer/id #uuid "79d24e48-d809-52e3-ad2f-fd1e75e9882a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264056} {:answer/id #uuid "aac71a88-0dd8-5c6f-afdc-8df09b432ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264462} {:answer/id #uuid "ed152b32-61af-5f9e-b9dd-61f70984cef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14df6deb-d3ee-5e1a-ad59-ff7cc32a5d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263194} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f7c60c4-fc52-503f-8ef4-67b53122d11d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "42eb872e-48fc-5f57-9397-6ecc0f021c29" :question/math-academy-id "q-130529" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(-12+2)+(9-5)+(-6+20) \\\\  & =20+4+14 \\\\  & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=\\frac{1}{38}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e9a7a36-a333-5a73-a6f8-e2d1a0a39a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{42}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263393} {:answer/id #uuid "7d53f47d-a7d0-59bb-b8f8-0e9e0f14d3c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264080} {:answer/id #uuid "9006f4e2-501c-5255-8645-a0e20a8bc8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264234} {:answer/id #uuid "95412705-5f71-5fb8-8941-52c8f2ca7268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264278} {:answer/id #uuid "cfbd30aa-8f8f-58dc-9aba-c954e828fb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{46}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfbd30aa-8f8f-58dc-9aba-c954e828fb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{46}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264820} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8aa345-1680-55b9-9d85-b18f97d1bceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33302e24-2ebe-5f9a-9cc7-6d4f67c8f493" :question/math-academy-id "q-130629" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (3+20) \\\\  & =-46\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{46}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f66b246-867d-5382-b745-224f4b0b1960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-276" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420805} {:answer/id #uuid "6f34b981-25b3-5127-9dcc-d184355dec85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "429" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420814} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaccfd8-0fee-5b02-b497-78e555afcf98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "294" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420826} {:answer/id #uuid "dd980cfa-ae7f-54ba-bba4-12f039277176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-372" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420835} {:answer/id #uuid "e9605807-1150-5819-807b-86c3f17e6ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-346" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd980cfa-ae7f-54ba-bba4-12f039277176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-372" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420835} :answer-field/id #uuid "96ea088a-9b87-51d6-9259-b1c59aec445a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c3dc4f1-ad56-595b-a7eb-41a85271c7fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-130693" :question/problem "Find\n$|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 114 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 114 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113+1 & 210+0 & 198+0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113+1 & 210+0 & 198+0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}| & =0+1(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 210 & 198\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4\\cdot 198-2\\cdot 210) \\\\  & =-(792-420) \\\\  & =-372\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43320e29-d3b8-528a-8fe9-99c0207ced61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572905} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3bb88c-6bbf-566b-9af8-cdfbef08a6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572913} {:answer/id #uuid "6070775f-39d6-5679-aa83-fc2bb298d71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572936} {:answer/id #uuid "7616d5ee-76de-57d2-9373-45d8ff05d835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572973} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e6ddf2-83fa-5605-bf51-12c940470cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-114" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d3bb88c-6bbf-566b-9af8-cdfbef08a6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572913} :answer-field/id #uuid "c090ec9d-391d-5fc2-a3e8-fc77e254c9bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5dad7293-4ccc-5f12-9124-fcdf29a624d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42576" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(4\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 5)\\cdot (1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-40)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336cb96e-8d35-5f9f-92e4-bf01f1fe608d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420806} {:answer/id #uuid "5725181a-ff3e-5107-8722-cd79c0a511e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420811} {:answer/id #uuid "b75e75d9-4872-5fbc-a59d-3e54eebab508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420824} {:answer/id #uuid "bb525c0d-2a19-503f-b30d-3b12439ddf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420825} {:answer/id #uuid "cab0cf74-f931-5eb5-9974-e3db30298fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb525c0d-2a19-503f-b30d-3b12439ddf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420825} :answer-field/id #uuid "75968549-80b9-5200-918a-66a961c78ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b58d794-d6a1-55fe-88d5-660b59bdd30a" :question/math-academy-id "q-42578" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =20-18 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59f38619-20b2-5322-be74-7ade54b5a4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572927} {:answer/id #uuid "69646748-f284-5f48-8b8c-5eab9d02207a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572952} {:answer/id #uuid "921c084b-39dd-579f-a483-01d5fac78c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573013} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bd6575-c2b6-5ac7-a9ee-1355aa285156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573051} {:answer/id #uuid "b98f6385-ee75-53c7-8fb6-adbddd6958be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bd6575-c2b6-5ac7-a9ee-1355aa285156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573051} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f31fc24-9a16-583f-8c7d-7e52bda34673" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59d8b4d6-365d-545b-a80d-dc97dcfbcac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-42997" :question/problem "Let $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ be square matrices. If $A=B\\cdot C,$  $|B|=9$ and $|{C}^{-1}|=-3$, find $|A|.$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nAlso, given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $M,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({M}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(M)}.\n$$\n\nSince $|C|=\\frac{1}{|{C}^{-1}|}$, we get $|C|=-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|B|\\cdot |C| \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15bb6772-ebfa-5300-9707-f0c44ba3ca38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420801} {:answer/id #uuid "1657176b-b7bb-5bac-8681-50357708185d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420802} {:answer/id #uuid "341db512-89dd-5d60-9521-449fcc83f4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420807} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecdfc0d-790a-509f-8041-8c18f54a719a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420817} {:answer/id #uuid "bd130b49-aec7-5947-9ffc-20dcb30a8a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd130b49-aec7-5947-9ffc-20dcb30a8a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420827} :answer-field/id #uuid "a81cf8c4-10bc-54bc-9308-65fe3b30e2ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e9ddda3-97aa-551d-9453-cae76713ec59" :question/math-academy-id "q-43070" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =10-5 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e097d8-e542-5acf-a330-fe6eeb6f27a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326952} {:answer/id #uuid "138116bf-b723-50e7-9f4c-0360ed73a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-434" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327101} {:answer/id #uuid "a0898f35-ae35-5a2c-85c3-2c1973fb0a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "870" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328324} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d333b3-316e-527a-9482-d8e7753af3e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328390} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b3cbac-6be7-58e9-bfd2-408356f2bda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-424" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "138116bf-b723-50e7-9f4c-0360ed73a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-434" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327101} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a480665-7d5c-59ec-8577-d65fbb82b058" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6ac6b8ae-6cc0-5802-bd63-e9b3982c4a3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-43097" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107+0 & 215+0 & 99+1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107+0 & 215+0 & 99+1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 99\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 99\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0+1(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =107\\cdot 8-215\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-434\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42489deb-4876-5c52-acde-7a5003dbd000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572903} {:answer/id #uuid "547ea20b-cbd8-5f06-a053-9482d610beaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572920} {:answer/id #uuid "d95a194b-3c11-588f-8353-b20c0750ef15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573099} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5dd8ca-3bb1-5003-b8a7-af27e1771acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573105} {:answer/id #uuid "e71150f4-7a00-5e7a-a36e-a900ab926a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd5dd8ca-3bb1-5003-b8a7-af27e1771acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573105} :answer-field/id #uuid "24bab1b2-9f01-5a84-84a1-c134dbb4356a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40a39262-0d1b-5421-8009-5952de54d116" :question/math-academy-id "q-88286" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 2(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1-2)\\cdot 2(1+2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47596227-58dd-5c85-b31b-dd2085e9d721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352226} {:answer/id #uuid "555a8298-192e-50be-8d44-14723e16f11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352323} {:answer/id #uuid "58619be6-c805-5344-9021-173a95976dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352344} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9db913-3c22-5c37-be9e-ca617e5d0dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353213} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c2b5b7-b8a6-51db-98b0-9f4ba4bb301f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47596227-58dd-5c85-b31b-dd2085e9d721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352226} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd53aab-78d5-531e-a248-9e6ce0d0938b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31688145-09ed-5774-a522-d76e627c7dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-88296" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=4,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{4}+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a4042a-f605-574f-a228-ff4ae6d308e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352358} {:answer/id #uuid "73d447eb-1e81-51dc-b291-5725cee52d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352581} {:answer/id #uuid "74558c61-b294-5a8c-be71-44c0d3822c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352586} {:answer/id #uuid "b27eb1ec-b060-5c68-b2a6-225457f0de0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353093} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c62a94-26b3-5bea-ae7b-f2fbef1384ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74558c61-b294-5a8c-be71-44c0d3822c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352586} :answer-field/id #uuid "607dd21d-aeb7-5920-91bc-f439f202ce2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27889ca3-5f85-5eb8-aee5-93c6c3d6c2ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-88298" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b+3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=5,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}(a+2) & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3) & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{5}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256655f5-3716-5c72-b038-769fa376cf93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420804} {:answer/id #uuid "65294e4c-a8db-5c12-befc-326f1199fa33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420812} {:answer/id #uuid "6c92f4c3-474c-5da4-bd3e-a7ddf9353c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420813} {:answer/id #uuid "b440a564-08a1-5c84-a502-ec561384a59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420821} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f48478-6c84-5d48-9002-c26925eb220f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "256655f5-3716-5c72-b038-769fa376cf93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420804} :answer-field/id #uuid "db1d1fba-e4ba-50df-bd35-d24e24ca86ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80589b9c-bfb0-57bc-8e70-de307d877343" :question/math-academy-id "q-88306" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $2$nd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300+0 & 250+20 & 100+0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300+0 & 250+20 & 100+0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0+20(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =20\\cdot (300\\cdot 1-100\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =4\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
